WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
I don't want to spend a ton of time on this today,&nbsp;
but I got a bunch of emails from people since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.760
Joe Biden was sworn in about what is likely to&nbsp;
happen from the standpoint of Donald Trump second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:15.760
impeachment trial, which we still don't know when&nbsp;
it will actually take place. But I think it makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.240
sense, just as a reminder that, yes, Donald Trump&nbsp;
was impeached a second time before leaving office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.080
in the House of Representatives. Yes, it will&nbsp;
be up to the Senate to vote to either convict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:32.000
or acquit in a trial that is going to take place&nbsp;
at some point in time. And it bears mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
that Republican support for convicting Donald&nbsp;
Trump in the Senate trial grew over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.240
few days of Trump's administration and during the&nbsp;
first few days of Joe Biden's. There's a Politico&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:51.120
morning consult poll released earlier this week&nbsp;
where about 20 percent of Republicans now say they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:58.560
either strongly or somewhat approve of conviction&nbsp;
of Donald Trump. That's up from the previous poll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.600
where only 14 percent of Republicans said the&nbsp;
same thing. So support for a conviction among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.760
Republican voters is up, but most Republicans&nbsp;
are still against conviction. And, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.040
it remains to be seen to what degree Republicans&nbsp;
continue to stand behind the now former president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.760
Donald Trump. It's very partizan. Still, 86&nbsp;
percent of Democrats approve of a conviction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:30.960
50 percent of independents strongly or somewhat&nbsp;
approve of a conviction, and again, 20 percent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.480
Republicans. But really what it's going to come&nbsp;
down to are Republican senators and it is mere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.040
speculation at this point. Would Mitt Romney&nbsp;
vote to convict? Mitt Romney voted to convict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.920
on one of two counts in Donald Trump's first&nbsp;
impeachment trial. Mitch McConnell reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.840
was pleased with the impeachment of Trump,&nbsp;
meaning what the House of Representatives did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:02:01.360
presumably believed to be because it allowed&nbsp;
the Republican Party to try to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.560
distance between itself and the then outgoing, now&nbsp;
former president. But just because Mitch McConnell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.760
is pleased that Trump was impeached in the House&nbsp;
doesn't mean Mitch McConnell has any plan to vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.160
to convict during the Senate impeachment trial&nbsp;
of Donald Trump. So it simply has to happen now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:25.520
a few just very, very basic ideas about this.&nbsp;
Some have floated the idea that we forget about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.320
the impeachment trial. I think this would be&nbsp;
a disaster if you do a if the House impeaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.760
they did it because they felt they had to.&nbsp;
That's the way impeachment is supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.600
work. It's a responsibility. The president does&nbsp;
something worthy of impeachment. Then you impeach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.640
It would send a very bad signal about the merely&nbsp;
political motivations which the motivations are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:53.440
Trump did something impeachable. It will not look&nbsp;
good if you do the impeachment in the House and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.440
then never do the trial. I think that that would&nbsp;
be an absolute disaster. And it opens up the door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.880
to impeachment becoming a much more political&nbsp;
thing. You know, when Marjorie Taylor Green, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.960
kooky kuhnen congresswoman, said, I'm going to&nbsp;
write up articles of impeachment for Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.560
before Biden was even sworn in, that's the type of&nbsp;
stuff that will start to see more on the level of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.320
what happened to Donald Trump in the House a week&nbsp;
and a half ago. If there is no trial, there must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.920
be a trial. The full process must take place in&nbsp;
terms of when I actually think sooner is better. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.600
think the longer you wait, the more the the lower&nbsp;
the chance of a conviction goes. So I would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.000
to see it soon. The Senate can walk and chew&nbsp;
gum at the same time. Joe Biden can get things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.800
done even while an impeachment trial is going&nbsp;
on. That's my view. I'd love to hear from you.

